# cube.json's BYTES are load-bearing, not just its content.
#
# D-41 requires the cube byte-reproducible from its header by a reader who has
# neither this environment nor its lockfile, and Sprint 4A's hardest done-when
# is that two runs produce byte-identical files. A Git end-of-line conversion
# on checkout defeats both silently: the file's content survives, its sha256
# does not, and nothing in the repo would notice.
#
# Found at the Sprint 4A review. The same cube came out 23,216 bytes on Linux
# and 24,280 on Windows -- exactly 1,064 bytes against 1,064 lines. spike_grid's
# write_cube() now writes BYTES rather than text, which pins the write side.
# This pins the checkout side. Both are needed; either alone still loses.
#
# Scoped narrowly ON PURPOSE. The captured measurement outputs under scripts/
# already carry CRLF in this repo and that is fine -- they are notes, not
# artifacts anyone hashes. Normalizing them would rewrite committed history for
# no gain, which is the kind of tidy-up this project declines elsewhere.
#
# cube_reference.json is the SAME cube produced on a different OS -- the agent's
# Linux container writes it, this machine writes cube.json, and the two sha256
# sums are compared. That IS the cross-machine gate, so it is the one file in
# the repo whose entire job is to be hashed against another file. It needs this
# pin MORE than cube.json does, not less: unpinned, a Windows checkout converts
# it to CRLF and the comparison fails against a correctly-written cube.json --
# the gate reporting a defect it invented.
cube.json           text eol=lf
cube_reference.json text eol=lf
